# Every path has human owners, and no agent is among them directly or through
# team membership.
#
# This file is a security control, not routing convenience (SPEC §10.1
# requirement 3). BEN's agents publish with a credential holding pull-request
# write, which is the permission needed to *open* a pull request — and the same
# permission lets that identity submit an approving review on a pull request it
# did not open. Such an approval counts toward `required_approving_review_count`,
# and satisfies `require_last_push_approval` too, because the agent was not the
# pusher. On a human-authored or second-agent-authored PR, that is a merge with
# nothing human-reviewed about it.
#
# Paired with the branch rule's `require_code_owner_reviews`, this file is what
# makes the required approval one no agent credential can produce: the agent is
# neither an owner of any path nor a member of an owner team, so its approval
# never satisfies the requirement no matter whose pull request it lands on.
#
# Two consequences worth knowing before editing:
#
#   * The wildcard must stay comprehensive. Code-owner review is only required
#     for paths that HAVE an owner, so any path this file does not cover is a
#     path an agent's own approval can carry. Narrowing `*` re-opens the hole
#     for whatever falls outside it.
#   * Never add a machine account or an App here or to an owner team. Team
#     membership is part of this control: every owner must be a principal BEN
#     cannot authenticate as.
#
# GitHub evaluates CODEOWNERS from the *base* branch, so a pull request that
# edits this file is still gated by the version already on `main`.

